There are absolute constants $a_*\in(0,1/2)$ and $C_*<\infty$ such that every Lipschitz field $v:\mathbb R^2\to S^1$ with $\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(v)\le1$ satisfies $$\sup_{0<\epsilon<a_*}
 \left\|H_{v,a_*}^{\epsilon}f\right\|_{L^2(\mathbb R^2)}
 \le C_*\left\|f\right\|_{L^2(\mathbb R^2)}
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^2)).$$ Its symmetric principal value therefore satisfies $$\left\|H_{v,a_*}f\right\|_2\le C_*\left\|f\right\|_2,
 \qquad
 \big|\{x:|H_{v,a_*}f(x)|>\lambda\}\big|
 \le C_*^2\lambda^{-2}\left\|f\right\|_2^2
 \quad(\lambda>0).$$

For $\left|t\right|\,C_{\rm Lip}<1$, define $$A_t h(x,y)=h(x-t,y-tu(x,y)).$$ For $1\le p<\infty$, $$\label{red:translate-bound}
 \left\|A_t h\right\|_p
   \le (1-\left|t\right|\,C_{\rm Lip})^{-1/p}\left\|h\right\|_p,$$ and $A_t$ is a contraction on $L^\infty$. Consequently, replacing either symmetric endpoint at scale $r$ by a measurable endpoint between $c r$ and $C r$, with $c,C>0$ fixed and $\max(1,C)r C_{\rm Lip}<1/2$, costs a bounded $L^p$ operator, independently of $r$.

For the fixed absolute constants in <a href="#red:local-operator" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="red:local-operator">[red:local-operator]</a>, there is an absolute $C$ such that, for every field $u$ with $\left|u\right|\le1$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(u)\le C_{\rm Lip}$, every $0<\epsilon<a$, every dyadic $w$, and every vertical band cutoff $P_w$, $$\label{eq:src-1}
 \left|\langle H_{u,a}^{\epsilon}P_w f,g\rangle\right|
 \le C\bigl(1+\left|\log(|F|/|E|)\right|\bigr)^{-3}
       \sqrt{|F||E|}$$ whenever $F,E\subset I\times\mathbb R$ have positive finite measure, $\left|f\right|\le\mathbf 1_F$, $\left|g\right|\le\mathbf 1_E$, and $\left|I\right|\le b_0$.

Suppose a bilinear form $\mathcal B$ satisfies $$\left|\mathcal B(f,g)\right|
 \le C_0(1+|\log(|F|/|E|)|)^{-3}\sqrt{|F||E|}
 \quad (|f|\le\mathbf 1_F,\ |g|\le\mathbf 1_E)$$ on finite-measure sets in a fixed measurable domain. Then $$\left|\mathcal B(f,g)\right|\le C C_0\left\|f\right\|_2\left\|g\right\|_2$$ for finite amplitude decompositions, and hence for all $L^2$ inputs whenever the form is obtained by an $L^2$-continuous truncation or by completion from those inputs.

For every fixed integer $c$, every $q\in L^\infty(\mathbb R)$, and every $h\in L^2(\mathbb R)$, $$\label{red:carleson-estimate}
 \sum_k\left\|(\Delta_kq)S_{k+c}h\right\|_2^2
      \le C_c\left\|q\right\|_\infty^2\left\|h\right\|_2^2.$$

Let $b\in W^{1,\infty}(\mathbb R)$, and let $T=\mathfrak m(D)$, where $\mathfrak m$ is smooth away from zero and $$A(\mathfrak m)
   =\max_{0\le r\le4}\sup_{\zeta\ne0}
        |\zeta|^r|\mathfrak m^{(r)}(\zeta)|<\infty.$$ Then $$\label{red:commutator-estimate}
 \left\|[b,T]\partial h\right\|_2
    \le C A(\mathfrak m)\left\|b'\right\|_\infty\left\|h\right\|_2.$$ The commutator is initially defined on the Sobolev space $W^{1,2}(\mathbb R)$; the estimate gives its bounded extension to $L^2$. Its constant is independent of $\left\|b\right\|_\infty$ and of all frequency truncations.

For $\left|t\right|\le b_0$ and $T$ as in [red:commutator](#red:commutator), $$\label{red:composition-bound}
 \left\|TC_t h-C_tTh\right\|_2
      \le C A(\mathfrak m)\left|t\right|\,\left\|h\right\|_2.$$ The constant is uniform over $\left|u\right|\le1$, $\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(u)\le C_{\rm Lip}$.

For dyadic $0<r\le R$, $$\label{red:scale-kernel-error}
 \int_\mathbb R\left|
    \frac{\mathbf 1_{\{r<|t|<R\}}}{t}
           -\sum_{r\le\ell\le R}k_\ell(t)
       \right|\,\mathrm dt\le C.$$ Also, $$\label{red:scale-first-moment}
 \int_\mathbb R|t|\left|\sum_{r\le\ell\le R}k_\ell(t)\right|
        \,\mathrm dt\le C R.$$ Both constants are independent of the number of scales. Changing either spatial endpoint by a fixed factor preserves the first bound.

Let the bin Hilbert space be $\mathcal H=\mathbb C^J$, where $J\leq C_{\rm bin}m$. If $\left\|g(z)\right\|_{\mathcal H}\leq\mathbf 1_E(z)$ and $D_E(s)\leq\gamma$ for every $s\in\mathcal S$, with $0<\gamma\leq C$, then $$\label{eq:src-4}
 |\Lambda_{\mathcal S}(f,g)|
 \leq C(1+\log(2m))^C\gamma^{1/4}
             |E|^{1/2}\left\|f\right\|_{L^2(\mathcal H)}.$$ In the scalar case, if $|f|\leq\mathbf 1_F$, $|g|\leq\mathbf 1_E$, and $0<|E|<|F|<\infty$, then $$\label{tile:eq-reverse-disparity}
 |\Lambda_{\mathcal S}(f,g)|
 \leq C\sqrt{|F||E|}
                \left(\frac{|E|}{|F|}\right)^{9/20}.$$ Both conclusions hold uniformly for every subcollection of $\mathcal S$.

Let $\mathcal T$ be separated tiles satisfying <a href="#tile:eq-tree" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tile:eq-tree">[tile:eq-tree]</a>, and let $\Upsilon_s$ be either of their packet families $\Phi_s$ or $\Psi_s$. For every fixed $J_0$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\|\mathfrak p_{T,J_0}
                \sum_{s\in\mathcal T}c_s\Upsilon_s\right\|_2
 &\leq C_{J_0}
          \left(\sum_{s\in\mathcal T}|c_s|^2|R_s|\right)^{1/2},
                  \label{tile:eq-synthesis}\\
 \left\|\mathfrak p_{T,J_0}
       \sup_{a<b}\left|
        \sum_{\substack{s\in\mathcal T\\a<\ell_s\leq b}}
                  c_s\Upsilon_s\right|\right\|_2
 &\leq C_{J_0}
          \left(\sum_{s\in\mathcal T}|c_s|^2|R_s|\right)^{1/2}.
                  \label{tile:eq-maximal-synthesis}
\end{aligned}$$ The same statements hold for Hilbert-valued sums $\bigl(\sum_s c_{s,j}\Upsilon_{s,j}\bigr)_j$, with $\sum_s\left\|c_s\right\|_{\mathcal H}^2|R_s|$ on the right and constants independent of $J$. Bounded scalar factors depending on $s,j$, such as $b_{s,j}(t)$, can be incorporated into the coefficients.

Suppose a scalar tree $\mathcal T$ satisfies $D_E(s)\leq\eta$ on its tiles and has size at most $\sigma$. If $|g|\leq\mathbf 1_E$, then $$\label{tile:eq-tree-bound}
 |\Lambda_{\mathcal T}(f,g)|
           \leq C\sigma\eta|R_T|.$$ The size assumption can be replaced by the same bound on any encompassing collection.

For the tops in <a href="#forest:eq-witnesses" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="forest:eq-witnesses">[forest:eq-witnesses]</a>, $$\label{eq:src-6}
 \left\|n\right\|_{L^{2,\infty}(\mathcal D)}
       \le C L^C\sqrt S.$$ More precisely, if $$D_*\ge\max(2K_0,\,2L^{C_{\mathrm{as}}},\,2),$$ then one may take the right-hand side to be $C L^{C_{\mathrm{as}}}D_*^3\sqrt S$, provided $D_*$ is chosen within an absolute factor of the displayed maximum. In particular, when $K_0=L^k$, a permissible exponent in <a href="#eq:src-6" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src-6">[eq:src-6]</a> is $$\label{forest:eq-count-exponent}
 c_{\mathrm{cnt}}(k)
     =C_{\mathrm{as}}+3\max(k,C_{\mathrm{as}}).$$

For a fixed scale $\ell=w/d$, and any subcollection of tiles satisfying <a href="#eq:src-9" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src-9">[eq:src-9]</a> at this scale, $$\label{conc:original-positive}
 \sum_{\ell_s=\ell}\left\|a_s\right\|_{\mathcal H}
       \mathcal B_{s,n}^{t}(z)\leq C_n.$$ There are fixed constants $0<c<1<C'$ for which $$\begin{aligned}
 f_d(z)&=C\left(
 \mathbf 1_{\{cd\leq\left|u(z)-t\right|\leq C'B^4d\}}
 +\frac{\left|u(z)-t\right|}{d}
       \mathbf 1_{\{\left|u(z)-t\right|\leq cd\}}\right),
       \label{conc:error-input}\\
 \sum_{\ell_s=\ell}\left\|a_s^{\rm err}\right\|_{\mathcal H}
       \mathcal B_{s,n}^{t}(z)
 &\leq C_n
 \big(k_{\ell,n'}*_{x} k_{W,n'}*_{Y_t}f_d\big)(z)
       \label{conc:error-majorant}
\end{aligned}$$ for a fixed $n'>2$ with $2n'<n$. The constants are independent of the number of labels permitted in <a href="#eq:src-9" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src-9">[eq:src-9]</a>.

For every $t$-box $Q$ of width $W$, the main coefficients satisfy $$\label{eq:src-10}
 \sum_{\substack{z_s\in Q\\\ell_s\leq\mathrm{length}(Q)}}
       \left\|a_s^{\rm main}\right\|_{\mathcal H}^2\left|R_s\right|
       \leq C\left|Q\right|.$$ The same estimate holds after restricting to any fixed subcollection.

Suppose $F_k\in L^2(\mathbb R;\mathcal H)$ has Fourier support in $\{\nu:c_a2^k\leq\left|\nu\right|\leq C_a2^k\}$, with fixed positive $c_a,C_a$. Then $$\label{conc:annular-var-bound}
 \left\|\mathcal V_3(F_k)\right\|_{L^2(\mathbb R)}
 \leq C_{c_a,C_a}
       \left(\sum_k\left\|F_k\right\|_2^2\right)^{1/2}.$$ The constant is independent of the finite index range and of $\dim\mathcal H$.

For every fixed $J_*>0$ there are a fixed exponent $C_{\mathrm{top}}$ and a measurable set $\mathcal X_{\rm top}$ with $\left|\mathcal X_{\rm top}\right|\leq S e^{-J_*L}$ such that, for all tops $T$ and all $z\notin\mathcal X_{\rm top}$, $$\label{eq:src-11}
 \sup_{\mathcal J}\ \sup_{\left|v\right|\leq W}
 \left(\left\|F_{T,\mathcal J}(z+(0,v))\right\|_{\mathcal H}
       +\left\|\mathcal D F_{T,\mathcal J}(z+(0,v))\right\|_{\mathcal H}\right)
 \leq L^{C_{\mathrm{top}}}\big(h_T^{(1)}(z)\big)^8.$$ Independently of this exceptional set, for every $z$, every $l_*>0$, and every interval $\mathcal J\subset[l_*,\infty)$, $$\label{conc:top-horizontal-derivative}
 \left\|(\partial_x+s_T\partial_y)F_{T,\mathcal J}(z)\right\|_{\mathcal H}
 \leq \frac{L^{C_{\mathrm{top}}}}{l_*}\big(h_T^{(1)}(z)\big)^8.$$ All packet orders, $K_1$, and $C_{\mathrm{top}}$ can be fixed before $A$; they are independent of the later mass cutoff and hierarchy depth.

Given any fixed $H>0$, choose $A$ sufficiently large after $K_1$ and $C_{\mathrm{top}}$, and then choose a sufficiently large fixed count threshold exponent $C_n$. There is a measurable set $F_{\rm conc}\subset F$ such that $$\left|F\setminus F_{\rm conc}\right|\leq L^{-H}\left|F\right|,
 \qquad
 \int_{F_{\rm conc}}\left\|P-P_q\right\|_{\mathcal H}
       \leq L^{-H}N\left|F\right|,
 \qquad P_q=\sum_sq_{T(s)}\phi_s,$$ and <a href="#eq:src-11" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src-11">[eq:src-11]</a> holds there for every top. On this set, $$\label{eq:src-12}
 n(z)=\sum_T h_T(z)\leq NL^{C_n}.$$ One has $K_0=L^{A+2}>K_1$, so both <a href="#eq:src-11" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:src-11">[eq:src-11]</a> and <a href="#conc:top-horizontal-derivative" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="conc:top-horizontal-derivative">[conc:top-horizontal-derivative]</a> also hold with $h_T$ in place of $h_T^{(1)}$.

There is a fixed exponent $C_D$ with the following property. For every fixed $J>0$ and all sufficiently large $L$, there is a measurable set $\mathcal E_{\mathrm{count}}\subset\mathbb R^2$ such that $$\label{count:exceptional-budget}
 |\mathcal E_{\mathrm{count}}|\leq S e^{-JL}.$$ Define $$\label{count:good-set}
 Z_0=F_{\mathrm{conc}}\setminus\mathcal E_{\mathrm{count}},
 \qquad D=L^{C_D}.$$ For every $z\in Z_0$, every $\rho>0$, and every list $\mathcal L$ of tops significant at $z$ satisfying $$\label{count:list-hypotheses}
 \begin{split}
 &\max_{T\in\mathcal L}s_T-\min_{T\in\mathcal L}s_T\leq B\rho,
 \qquad d_T\leq\rho/B^2\quad(T\in\mathcal L),\\
 &|s_T-s_{T'}|\geq\rho\quad(T,T'\in\mathcal L,\ T\ne T'),
 \end{split}$$ one has $\#\mathcal L\leq D/100$. The exponent $C_D$ depends only on the fixed geometric constants, including the already chosen mask exponent $A$. It is independent of the number of tops, the number of scales, and the bin Hilbert space.

Let $(f_b)_{b\in\mathcal B}$ be a countable family of measurable functions on $\mathbb R$ such that $0\leq f_b\leq1$ and $\sum_b f_b\leq C_0$, where $C_0\geq1$. Fix $H>0$ and $0<\vartheta\leq1$. There is a measurable function $\mathcal N:\mathbb R\to[0,\infty]$ with the following properties. If $\mathcal B_x$ is any collection of distinct indices such that, for every $b\in\mathcal B_x$, some interval $I_b$ containing $x$ satisfies $$\label{count:line-average}
 0<|I_b|\leq H,\qquad |I_b|^{-1}\int_{I_b}f_b\geq\vartheta,$$ then $\#\mathcal B_x\leq\mathcal N(x)$. Moreover, for every bounded interval $U$ and every $\lambda\geq0$, $$\label{count:line-tail}
 |\{x\in U:\mathcal N(x)>\lambda\}|
 \leq C(|U|+H)\exp(-c\vartheta\lambda/C_0),$$ where $C,c>0$ are absolute. If the $f_b$ depend jointly measurably on an additional parameter, $\mathcal N$ can be chosen jointly measurable in that parameter and $x$.

On $Z_0$, for $0\le\Re\zeta_\sigma\le\mu$, $$\left\|A_v(\zeta,z)\right\|_{\mathcal H}
 \le L^C(1+\left\|\zeta\right\|_2)n_v(z).
 \label{eq:src-17}$$ More precisely, the contribution of any one eligible top $T$ to $A_v$, also when restricted to any interval of packet lengths, is bounded by $$L^C(1+\left\|\zeta\right\|_2)|q_T(z)|h_T(z)^4.
 \label{hier:individual-amplitude}$$ If all eligible tops of $v$ are insignificant at $z\in Z_0$, then $$\left\|A_v(\zeta,z)\right\|_{\mathcal H}
 \le L^C(1+\left\|\zeta\right\|_2)e^{-L\log L}n_v(z).
 \label{hier:insignificant-amplitude}$$

In the setting of [hier:mass-motion](#hier:mass-motion), fix a top $T$ contributing to $A_c$, and take endpoint weights $\omega_k=\omega_{T,i_{j+1},k}$. If $z_0\in I\cap Z_0$ and the parameters satisfy <a href="#hier:vertex-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hier:vertex-parameters">[hier:vertex-parameters]</a>, then, uniformly for $z'\in I$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \left\|(\omega_k(z'))_k\right\|_{\mathrm{BV}}
 &\le L^C h_T(z_0)^{-4},
 \label{hier:moving-weight-bv}\\
 \left\|(\omega_k(z')-\omega_k(z_0))_k\right\|_{\mathrm{BV}}
 &\le G^{-1/2}L^C h_T(z_0)^{-4}.
 \label{hier:moving-weight-difference}
\end{aligned}$$ Both conclusions hold for any interval of endpoints. Neither assumes that $z'$ belongs to $Z_0$.

Fix a block child $c$ of $J$, a path top $R$ eligible in $J$, and an $H_j$ cell on a line of slope $s_R$. Let $t_c$ be the angular center of $c$. If $z_0\in Z_0$ lies in that cell and $z'=z_0+(b,s_Rb)$ is any other point in the cell, then, for the parameters <a href="#hier:vertex-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hier:vertex-parameters">[hier:vertex-parameters]</a>, $$\left\|A_c(\zeta,z')-\mathcal D_c(b)A_c(\zeta,z_0)\right\|_{\mathcal H}
 \le G^{-c_0}n_c(z_0),
 \qquad
 \mathcal D_c(b)_{\nu\nu}
 =\exp\bigl(i(s_R-t_c)\eta_\nu b\bigr).
 \label{eq:src-21}$$ Here $c_0>0$ is absolute and may also be used, after decreasing it, in <a href="#hier:complex-transition-motion" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="hier:complex-transition-motion">[hier:complex-transition-motion]</a>. The estimates hold at all displaced points in the cell, not just its points in $Z_0$.

For every nonempty root $v$, every shift $\sigma$, and every parameter with $$\Re\zeta=\mu e_\sigma,
 \qquad \|\Im\zeta\|_2\le L^2,$$ one has $$\label{eq:src-29}
 \big\|n_v^{\alpha/p-1}A_v(\zeta)\big\|_{L^p(Z;\mathcal H)}
       \le L^C S^{1/p}.$$ The constant and exponent are independent of the shift and the grid translation.

Let $\epsilon=4G^{-1/4}$. For $z\in Z_0$, $$\label{assembly:expected-error}
 \mathbb E_\vartheta\|P_q(z)-\mathcal W_\vartheta(z)\|
                    \le L^C\epsilon n(z).$$ Here the expectation is normalized Lebesgue measure for the common translation modulo $d(0)$; independent translations at different levels are neither assumed nor needed.
